Actions, in order:
1. kubectl taint nodes ekb-k8s-worker2 maintenance=true:NoExecute

Rules for these actions: A Service sends traffic only to ready pods whose labels match every key and value of its selector. When no ready pod matches, the Service has no ready endpoints and requests to it fail. A NoExecute taint on a node evicts the pods running there that do not tolerate it (a pod with a matching toleration stays, or leaves after its tolerationSeconds). NoSchedule only keeps new pods without a matching toleration from being scheduled there and does not evict running pods. An evicted pod's replacement goes to another node it is allowed to run on, or stays Pending if there is none.

The questions are about the state after all these actions.

