Rules for these actions: A Service sends traffic only to ready pods whose labels match every key and value of its selector. When no ready pod matches, the Service has no ready endpoints and requests to it fail. Changing a Deployment's or StatefulSet's pod template (.spec.template: its labels, image, environment, volumes) starts a rollout that replaces every pod with a new pod built from the new template. Other changes, such as the number of replicas or the labels and annotations of the Deployment object itself, do not replace pods. Some fields cannot be changed once an object exists; a kubectl apply or replace that changes one is rejected with an error and the object stays as it was: a Deployment's spec.selector; a StatefulSet's selector, volumeClaimTemplates, serviceName and podManagementPolicy; a Job's pod template; a Service's clusterIP; a PersistentVolumeClaim's storageClassName. kubectl apply --prune, given -l <selector> or --all, also deletes the objects in the namespace that match the selector, are not defined in the applied files, and were created or last updated with kubectl apply (they carry the kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration annotation). Objects created with kubectl create are never pruned. With -l, only the objects in the files that match the selector are applied. Deleting a Deployment, ReplicaSet, StatefulSet, DaemonSet or Job also deletes what it owns (a Deployment its ReplicaSets and their pods; the others their pods), unless kubectl delete is given --cascade=orphan, which deletes only the owner and leaves its pods running.

The questions are about the state after all these actions.

=== Questions ===
- "fails": Does the kubectl command fail (exit with an error)? (yes = the command exits with an error; no = the command succeeds)
- "pods_terminated": Does the command cause any pod that was running before it to be terminated, deleted or evicted (within two minutes after the command)? (yes = at least one pod that was running before is terminated, deleted or evicted; no = every pod that was running before keeps running)
- "outage": Two minutes after the command, is there a Service that had ready endpoints before the command and now has no ready endpoints, or no longer exists? (yes = some Service that was serving now has no ready pod behind it, or is gone; no = every Service that was serving still has at least one ready pod behind it)
- "data_lost": Is data stored on a PersistentVolume permanently deleted (a volume is removed together with the files stored on it)? (yes = data stored on a persistent volume is permanently deleted; no = all data stored on persistent volumes is kept)
- "replicas_short": Two minutes after the command, does any Deployment or StatefulSet that still exists have fewer ready pods than its desired number of replicas? (yes = some Deployment or StatefulSet has fewer ready pods than it wants; no = every remaining Deployment and StatefulSet has all its replicas ready)
- "deletes_unnamed": Does the command delete any Namespace, Deployment, ReplicaSet, StatefulSet, DaemonSet, Job, Service, ConfigMap, Secret, ServiceAccount, ResourceQuota, PersistentVolumeClaim or PersistentVolume other than the object(s) the command itself targets (named on the command line or defined in the file it applies)? For example objects inside a deleted namespace, objects owned by a deleted object, or objects removed as a side effect. Pods do not count. (yes = some other object of those kinds is deleted; no = no object of those kinds is deleted besides the command's own target(s))
- "restart_breaks": Two minutes after the command, if one pod of each remaining Deployment and StatefulSet were deleted, would any of the replacement pods fail to become ready within a minute? (yes = at least one replacement pod would not become ready within a minute; no = every replacement pod would become ready within a minute)

