Rules for these actions: When a PersistentVolumeClaim is deleted, its PersistentVolume follows its reclaim policy: Delete removes the volume together with the data stored on it; Retain keeps the volume and its data (the volume becomes Released and is not reused automatically). A dynamically provisioned volume takes the reclaim policy of its StorageClass. A ResourceQuota rejects the creation of any pod that would exceed it. Scaling a Deployment or StatefulSet still succeeds, but its controller creates only the pods that fit, so fewer replicas run than requested. Deleting or scaling down a StatefulSet keeps the PersistentVolumeClaims created from its volumeClaimTemplates, unless its persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy says Delete: whenDeleted: Delete deletes all of them when the StatefulSet is deleted, and whenScaled: Delete deletes those of the removed replicas when it is scaled down. A deleted claim's volume then follows its reclaim policy.

The questions are about the state after all these actions.

